"anchor_status":"valid","anchor_stability":"symbol","voice":"gpt-5.5-pro","answered_by":"codex-headless"},{"id":"DISS-C-001","severity":"HIGH","category":"authz","anchor":".claude/scripts/lib/dec-write-lib.sh:is_operator_call","anchor_type":"line","anchor_line":360,"anchor_line_end":360,"scope":"cross_file","trigger_anchor":".claude/commands/decisions.md:disable-model-invocation","cross_file_justification":"decisions.md states the whole point of the boundary: 'This command is the only caller of dec-write-lib.sh confirm ... because an agent that could run them would confirm its own decisions' and relies on `disable-model-invocation: true` plus a bats grep (tests/unit/dec-write-lib.bats) to hold that line. The writer itself has no stronger check than the flag.","description":"Operator authority is a self-asserted CLI string. `is_operator_call` is true whenever the caller passes `--recorded-by decisions-command`; that single flag unlocks `confirm` (line 509), `reject` on a decided row (line 575), operator-style `supersede` (lines 601/607) and `context --clear` (line 717). `disable-model-invocation` only stops the model from launching the /decisions slash command through the Skill tool; it does nothing to the Bash tool, which can execute `.claude/scripts/lib/dec-write-lib.sh confirm --id DEC-0007 --recorded-by decisions-command --evidence cli:2026-10-22T00:00:00Z --answer-ref cli:2026-10-22T00:00:00Z` directly. Because `cli:` matches OPREF_RE, the confirm path (line 539) then records `decided_by: operator`, so an agent can mint an operator-attributed decision for a row it proposed itself. The only runtime guard is `live_run_signal`, which is bypassable (see DEC-AUTHZ-002). The exact words of the design doc describe this as the threat the feature exists to prevent, and the full file (decisions.md, line 'Ledger rows are untrusted text written by agents') also means a prompt-injected row can steer a session into running exactly this command.","failure_mode":"An agent (or a